---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { houseRules } from '../data/site';
---
<Base title="How we work — Edge of the Coin">
  <section>
    <div class="stack col">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
      <h1>How we work</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Writing about politics and religion means being accused of bias by everyone, constantly
        and permanently. The defence is not protesting neutrality. It is writing the rules down
        in advance, in public, and being visibly bound by them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-mark">
      <p class="eyebrow">House rules</p>
      <span class="bar"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="rules">
      {houseRules.map((r) => (
        <div class="rule-row">
          <h3>{r.name}</h3>
          <p>{r.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-mark">
      <p class="eyebrow">Claim types</p>
      <span class="bar"></span>
    </div>
    <h2 class="col">Three kinds of claim</h2>
    <p class="col">
      This is the single most useful thing the site does. Most political and religious arguments
      are two people disputing incompatible values while both pretend to be disputing facts.
      Naming which is which defuses more than balanced prose ever will.
    </p>
    <div class="rules col">
      <div class="rule-row">
        <p><span class="tag empirical">Empirical</span></p>
        <p>A claim about the world. Carries a source, can be checked, and can be wrong.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule-row">
        <p><span class="tag values">Values</span></p>
        <p>A moral premise. No amount of data settles it. Labelling it stops you waiting for evidence that cannot exist.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule-row">
        <p><span class="tag predictive">Predictive</span></p>
        <p>A forecast about consequences. Reasonable now, possibly false later, and flagged so the page gets revisited.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="col epigraph">
      The tags differ in border — solid, dotted, dashed — not only in colour, so the distinction
      survives on a phone in sunlight, in print, and for a colourblind reader.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="col note">
      <p class="eyebrow">If we got your side wrong</p>
      <p>
        The steelman rule is the one we most want to be held to. If a position you hold is
        misrepresented here, tell us and it gets corrected in the open, with the change recorded
        on the page itself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
